WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.749
This used to be RAF Graveley,
a Royal Air Force base near Cambridge.

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:07.190
Now, it's a farm and some wind turbines,
but in the 1940s

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.670
aircraft would set off from here
to fight in Europe.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.170
And, they hoped, they'd be able to safely
land back here as well.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.030
The problem with that was the fog.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.980
Or, rather, smog: all the soot and air pollution
from domestic coal fires

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.199
combined with fog to make an incredibly thick
"pea soup".

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:23.750
It's never seen in modern Britain

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.560
because we don't burn smoke-producing coal
in every house any more.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.830
But back then, if that smog came in,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.360
even runway lights wouldn't get through it

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.190
and there were no modern instruments
to guide the air crews home.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.730
No way to take off, no way to land.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.100
How do you solve that?

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.960
By setting enormous amounts of petrol on fire.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.290
This incredible photo shows a Lancaster bomber
taking off from just behind me,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.430
more than 70 years ago.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:48.590
It looks like a movie stunt,

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.750
but this was how wartime airfields
boiled off fog.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.520
Set up enormous lines of petrol burners to
heat up the air,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.809
vaporise all the liquid water droplets to
make steam,

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:00.030
which will rise out of the way
and clear the air.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.830
The system was called FIDO.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.809
“Fog, Intensive, Dispersal Of.”

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:07.649
There are demonstration films taken by the
RAF on clear days,

00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:09.950
showing just how big
and how visible this was.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.740
There's a report in The War Illustrated,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.450
published just after victory in Europe,

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.219
that says that the glow from the fires was
visible for miles.

00:01:16.219 --> 00:01:19.149
There was a bit of smoke
when they started the system up,

00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:21.849
but once it got hot, the air could be so clear

00:01:21.849 --> 00:01:25.100
that people on the ground could see the stars.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.599
The catch was how expensive it was.

00:01:26.599 --> 00:01:30.700
A small Fido system burne
70,000 gallons of fuel an hour.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.009
One estimate says that in one foggy night
over England,

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:37.640
all the Fido systems in use
would burn 20 million gallons of fuel.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.649
There's even a report all the extra heat pumped
into the atmosphere triggered a thunderstorm.

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:44.509
At the current price of jet fuel,
as I record this,

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:49.659
running a Fido system would cost
about $200,000 per hour per runway.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:51.139
It was never used in peacetime:

00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:54.549
aircraft could just not take off instead
and wait for the fog to clear,

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:57.009
or divert to another airport if they were
trying to land.

00:01:57.009 --> 00:01:58.469
But for the war effort,

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:00.819
to let planes take off for battle in any weather

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:02.499
and to save thousands of air crew lives

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.840
who needed to land right now:

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:05.840
it was worth it.

